\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Charge, ionic paths, and Kirchhoff's law}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The charge separated by a patch of membrane is proportional to the
voltage across the patch.
\citet{(1.5), §1.4, p. 8}{ermentrout2010mathematical} writes
#{q=C_{M}V_{M}}, and
\citet{(5.2), §5.2, draft p. 5}{dayan2001theoretical} writes
#{C_{m}\dot V=\dot Q}. The current that changes the charge is
therefore proportional to #{\dot V}.}

\p{An ionic path across the same patch carries a current that vanishes
at one voltage and changes sign as the voltage passes that value.
\citet{(1.8), §1.4, p. 9}{ermentrout2010mathematical} is the potassium
current #{g_{\mathrm{K}}(V_{M}-E_{\mathrm{K}})}.
\citet{Ch. 6, p. 99}{nicholls2012neuron} describes the resting
membrane as a balance of fluxes: potassium tends to leave the cell,
sodium and chloride tend to enter, and the membrane potential opposes
those movements.}

\p{Kirchhoff's current law requires the currents into the patch to sum
to zero. The capacitive current, the ionic currents, and the current
applied through an electrode therefore determine #{\dot V}. The
circuit in
\citet{§1.4, pp. 8–10}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
is a capacitor, a conductor in series with a battery for each ionic
path, and a source for the applied current.}
